{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially in rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +2 EUR early horizon; larger further out).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing, the model lags and misses the magnitude of continuation. Add a momentum term proportional to recent slope (e.g., add 0.2–0.4×(last price − 10d MA) to days 1–10).",
    "Horizon-dependent underpricing: the error grows with forecast horizon (days 10–30 often several EUR too low). Use a ramping correction that increases with lead time (e.g., +1 EUR by day 10, +3 to +5 EUR by day 30 in strong uptrends).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls forecasts back toward the recent mean while the market continues trending upward. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is rising and the 10d MA is above the 30d MA.",
    "Post-dip rebound underestimated: after a sharp drawdown in history, the model expects continued weakness, but outcomes rebound more strongly. If last 10d return is negative but last 3d return turns positive, boost days 1–7 by an extra +0.5 to +1.5 EUR.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized swings; widen forecast distribution (or add a volatility uplift) when 5d realized vol exceeds 20d vol."
  ]
}
